Step 1: Access the Repository
The project is hosted on GitHub: https://github.com/Siddhesh-2028/CrashCrew
Ask them to clone it using: git clone https://github.com/Siddhesh-2028/CrashCrew.git
Step 2: Prerequisites
Ensure Docker Desktop is installed and running on their system (Windows, macOS, or Linux). Download from docker.com.
Step 3: Deployment Instructions (Using Docker Compose)
From the repository root directory, run these commands in a terminal (PowerShell on Windows):
# Navigate to the project folder
cd 'path\to\CrashCrew'  # Replace with the actual path after cloning

# Build and start the services in the background
docker-compose up --build -d

# (Optional) View logs to check for issues
docker-compose logs -f

# To stop and remove containers when done
docker-compose down
Backend will be available at: http://localhost:5000
Frontend will be available at: http://localhost:5173
Additional Notes
This is a full-stack app with a Vue.js frontend, Flask backend, and SQLite database, all containerized for easy deployment.
If they encounter issues (e.g., port conflicts), they can modify docker-compose.yml to change ports.
For production deployment (e.g., on a server), they may need to adjust configurations, add environment variables for secrets, or use a reverse proxy like Nginx. The Docker setup is a good starting point.
If they prefer running locally without Docker, they can install Node.js for the frontend (npm install && npm run dev) and Python for the backend (using the included .venv).
If the organizer needs more details or runs into problems, they can refer to the README_DOCKER.md and README.md files in the repo for additional context.
